# {{ project_name }}

Build and operate your startups in your own accounts, using shared knowledge,
automation and tooling from [Deploy Your Startup](https://deploy-your-startup.com).
This Factory starts with no personal startups. Public tooling repositories are
included so you can learn from them and contribute improvements.
Read [VISION.md](VISION.md) and [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md).

## Set up your workspace

The supported operational environment is macOS. Guided
[onboarding](https://deploy-your-startup.com/get-started) creates and sets up this
workspace automatically. To resume setup manually, use Startup CLI v0.1.52 or
newer, install the workspace tools and connect GitHub:

```bash
brew install mise yq gh
# Install uv if it is not already available; see https://docs.astral.sh/uv/
gh auth login -h github.com -p https -s repo,workflow,read:packages,write:packages
gh auth setup-git
startup doctor
mise trust
mise install
mise exec -- ./scripts/init.sh
```

Review `projects.yaml` first. Setup clones the public tooling over HTTPS; it does
not provision servers or deploy an application. Existing project directories are
preserved. If setup reports a clone failure, fix the error and rerun it. Agent
skill installation needs a configured coding agent; follow the printed guidance.
You do not need Kubernetes knowledge to test an idea: bootstrap, deployment and
landing pages work without `kubectl`. Install it (`brew install kubectl`) only
when you want to inspect a full-stack startup's cluster yourself.

Onboarding makes this workspace its own Git repository with a first commit and
offers a private GitHub backup. If it has no Git history or GitHub repository
yet, run `startup factory init --github --project-dir .` (Startup CLI v0.1.37 or
newer); it asks before committing and before creating the repository. Keep the
repository private: it holds your portfolio. Registered startups are committed
automatically; commit your other changes yourself.

## Agent skills

`scripts/init.sh` automatically installs the deployment, Sprowi task and idea
review skills for your configured Claude Code, Codex or OpenCode agents.
The deployment skill comes with the Startup CLI; the other two are installed
with `gh skills` from your local Factory files. If prompted, update
GitHub CLI. After setting up an agent later, rerun `scripts/init.sh`, then start
a new agent session.

Additional integrations from the [skills repository](https://github.com/Deploy-your-Startup/skills)
are optional and are not installed automatically.

## Add your first startup

From this workspace root, run:

```bash
startup bootstrap --output-dir projects/startups
```

Review the provider costs and complete the CLI's prompts. The CLI registers the
created Git repository in `projects.yaml`, including when setup needs to resume.
It records deployment status only after separate live verification.
For a repository created outside bootstrap, clone it under `projects/startups`
and run `startup factory register projects/startups/example-app`. The entry uses
its actual remote:

```yaml
projects:
  - name: example-app
    repo: https://github.com/example-owner/example-app.git
```

Record a project even when bootstrap needs to resume. Add `deployed: true`, `url`
and `kubectl_context` only after verifying Actions, DNS, HTTPS and cluster access.
For full-stack deployments, `startup verify --working-directory
projects/startups/example-app` checks the deployment. Follow AGENTS.md for legal
onboarding and security checks before public launch.

Claude Code offers to install the bundled
[dashboard mod](https://github.com/Deploy-your-Startup/startup-factory-mod) when you
trust this folder; `/factory` then shows each startup's website, pods and git state.

Run `mise tasks --all` to discover project commands. Use `scripts/sync.sh --project <name>` to sync a registered repository,
`--hub` for the hub, or `--all` for the full portfolio and registry check.
Successful runs print a compact summary; add `--verbose` for details.
Questions and planning do not require a sync. See [operations](docs/operations.md)
for local-change handling and registry drift.

## Keep the shared foundation up to date

From a clean, committed workspace, select a published release:

```bash
startup template update --version <release-tag> --dry-run
startup template update --version <release-tag>
```

Review the diff, resolve conflicts, run `mise run test` and commit the update with
`.copier-answers.yml`. Your startup registry and Sprowi settings are preserved.
Review release notes for optional changes to those owner-managed files; new
registry entries and Sprowi defaults are not applied automatically.

Update all registered startups from this workspace:

```bash
./scripts/update-templates.sh          # preview
./scripts/update-templates.sh --apply  # apply locally
```

Each startup's Copier metadata selects its template; the script uses its latest
HEAD. Missing checkouts, repositories without Copier metadata and uncommitted
changes are skipped. Review the diffs, resolve conflicts, run project checks and
commit each update. The script does not pull, commit, push or deploy.

Shared deployment updates include full-stack and Pages projects and require a
CLI v0.1.52 or newer with single-version deployment support. Older CLIs stop before changing files.
Roles and workflows are updated separately, one release at a time. Each startup stays on its release until you move it:

```bash
./scripts/update-deployments.sh          # show each startup's release
./scripts/update-deployments.sh --apply  # move clean checkouts to the latest
```

Nothing is deployed: commit and push a startup to roll its update out. In a
single startup, use `startup update --check`, `startup update` and
`startup update --version <release>` to go back.

Pulling this workspace with Git does not apply upstream Factory template changes.

## Code in a local sandbox

Install Docker Sandboxes and sign in with your Docker account. Set up the
interactive Codex login on the host once with `sbx secret set openai --oauth`.
Then pick one workspace from the Factory root.

```bash
./scripts/coding-sandbox.sh          # Factory hub
./scripts/coding-sandbox.sh my-app                # one registered project
./scripts/coding-sandbox.sh --agent claude my-app # Claude Code
```

Each session starts from the selected repository's committed snapshot. The
sandbox image includes the Factory's locked Startup CLI and a kubectl client.
Docker builds the tools kit on first use and reuses the build cache. To build
the kit separately, run `bash scripts/with-factory-kit.sh build`.
Cluster access requires a separate connection and credentials.

For live pod and deployment status, run
`bash scripts/with-factory-kit.sh status factory-live-status` on your Mac.
This creates a separate sandbox with the live Factory checkout and a
`cluster_status` tool. The tool uses existing host Kubernetes contexts,
accepts only a registered startup name, and returns health counts and names.
Its executable and project allowlist are stored outside the checkout.

For sessions started with `coding-sandbox.sh`, local edits, untracked files,
other checkouts and Git credentials stay on the host. Review the committed
snapshot before opening it in a sandbox, including any encrypted Vault files.
The starter disables shared skills and external MCP servers, skips SSH
agent forwarding and blocks GitHub and common deployment endpoints. For
dependencies, it uses your Docker Sandboxes network policy.

Claude Code uses its own account sign-in when prompted. When the agent exits,
use the printed `--diff` command to export a patch. Review it
before applying it to your checkout. Use `--prepare` to create a sandbox without
launching the agent. Local bootstrap and Vault operations use `startup` from your
own terminal with the project's configured credential backend.

## Optional agent loop

Run `scripts/init.sh --with-sprowi` only when you want the Sprowi agent loop. It needs
Docker Desktop, sbx and an engine account. See [docs/sprowi.md](docs/sprowi.md) for
setup, budgets, experiments and publishing with your own GitHub App. No App ID,
personal task history or production credentials are bundled.

For researched startup ideas, run `scripts/sprowi.sh ideas --engine claude`
(or `--engine codex`; `--for <project>` focuses on one startup). Three reviewed
ideas are added to `ideas/` and committed; nothing is built or deployed.
An optional macOS schedule works without Codex; see
[daily discovery](docs/sprowi.md#daily-discovery-with-claude-or-codex).
